COPY norm_runtime.background_memory_snapshots (id, summary, source_through, created_at) FROM stdin;
5	NORM GLOBAL BACKGROUND MEMORY â€” CONSOLIDATED 2026-09-17\n\nCURRENT ARCHITECTURE\n- KHzz is the current runtime source of truth. Norm uses Ollama for local inference, Redis/Memurai for disposable live execution state, and PostgreSQL as the durable ledger.\n- Internal control-plane protocol is JSON v2; user-facing replies remain normal English/Markdown. Final acceptance comes from parsed schema-validated verifier fields, not magic strings.\n- PostgreSQL terminal task state/summary must be durable before task-specific Redis cleanup. Startup/periodic maintenance reconciles Redis against PostgreSQL. Graceful shutdown drains work and clears transient runtime state.\n- `SOS.readme` is transient unresolved-task state. Remove it only when the matching originating task is durably completed or deliberately finalized failed with a useful terminal summary; cancelled/unresolved work does not clear it.\n\nOPERATOR / DEVELOPMENT RULES\n- For essentially every remote operation beyond a trivial one-liner, use `C:\\Users\\KHzz\\Documents\\Norm\\verbatim_lines.py` to create a temporary Python script, run it, verify results, then delete the temporary script. Avoid nested PowerShell quoting.\n- Stage substantial runtime changes, inspect and test source, package only after source validation, test the packaged candidate, back up before promotion, then promote through graceful shutdown/restart.\n- Routine code backups do not require PostgreSQL exports. Take a database backup only before intentionally destructive/risky memory or data work when preserving the pre-change state is useful.\n\nMEMORY / RETENTION POLICY\n- PostgreSQL is a knowledge base, not an immutable event warehouse. Raw chats, task runs, steps, evidence, verifier chatter, smoke tests, retries, and summaries are expendable once a denser verified record preserves what matters.\n- Permanently delete high-confidence redundant or superseded records. Do not keep dead rows merely marked superseded.\n- Repeated attempts of the same one-off request should collapse into one useful representative or one compact history note. Routine successful smoke/retry history has near-zero long-term value.\n- Summaries are also temporary layers: thread/task summaries may later be merged into project/topic summaries and then deleted. Preserve durable facts, user preferences, decisions, constraints, reusable failure lessons, artifact pointers/hashes, and unresolved follow-ups; discard narration and duplicated execution history.\n- Global background memory must stay small and cross-task. Detailed project facts belong in project/thread memory and should be retrieved only when relevant.\n\nTOOLS / CONNECTIONS\n- Connection checks are demand-driven. Do not probe optional resources globally just because they exist. Relative local-file storage prefers configured CA8D SMB when responsive and falls back to KHzz docs; unrelated paths/resources should not be queried.\n- Existing-file mutations require deterministic verification/read-back. Deletions are reversible/staged until the normal purge path where applicable.\n\nIMAGE / CHART LESSONS\n- Live image analysis uses the v3 layered/occlusion-aware analyzer. Separate constant/reference geometry from data objects and annotations; ambiguous high-impact regions deserve focused rescans rather than forced certainty.\n- Numeric chart price conversion must use visible labeled price anchors from the same pane. Pane edges, crop boundaries, separators, volume geometry, and unlabeled lines are never price anchors. Enforce monotonic/local-bracket consistency before accepting y->price conversion.\n- Corrective QQQ regression passed after rejecting a false pane-bottom anchor and volume-bar/wick confusion. SPY axis-calibration correction is settled: the cancelled above-750 interpretation is superseded by application-readout ground truth of 749.60 for the Sep 16 15:15 low; later full-image review also corrected the SPY 15m pane boundary to ~y=578 rather than the in-pane y~440 gridline.\n\nFUTURE ARCHITECTURE\n- A dedicated coordinator on another machine should normalize/classify requests, retrieve only relevant PostgreSQL/local-source context, submit work, monitor Redis, independently verify transitions/results, deliver the final reply, confirm transient cleanup, and persist the next dense summary.\n- Persistent local-source indexing and automated aggressive memory compaction/hard deletion remain future work. The desired steady state is bounded high-value knowledge, not unbounded raw history.	2026-09-17 10:58:40.347377-04	2026-09-20 12:10:15.776973-04
\.
