113	chat-1d9e33f3-cab0-44f3-8f7b-3cf32241dcf1	step-04	vision_image	{"paths": ["C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\norm images\\\\QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916.jpg", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\occlusion_crop_01_zoom4x.png", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\occlusion_crop_02_zoom4x.png", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\occlusion_crop_10_zoom4x.png", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\focus_scan_01_8x.png", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\focus_scan_02_8x.png"], "prompt": "This is a corrective audit of a QQQ chart. The disputed region is the deep red area around x≈510-552, y≈833-840 in the original image (image is 1217x1536). The axis mapping (verified from labeled anchors 709.18@y=298 and 704.69@y=514.5) gives: price(y) = 709.18 - (y-298)*0.020715. So y=833 → ~698.0, y=836 → ~697.9, y=840 → ~697.8, y=740 → ~700.0, y=735 → ~700.1.\\n\\nI need you to examine the zoomed crops (images 2-6) and the original (image 1) and answer these specific questions:\\n\\n1. In the zoomed crops of the disputed red region (x≈510-552, y≈833-840), do you see a THIN vertical red trace (1-3px wide) that is DISTINCT from and CONTINUES BELOW the broad red candle body/volume fill? Or is the bottom edge of the broad red fill simply the lowest red pixel, with no separate thin trace extending below it?\\n\\n2. Is the broad red region in this area a CANDLE BODY (price data) or a VOLUME BAR (volume data)? Look at its width, position relative to the price pane vs volume pane, and shape.\\n\\n3. In the original image (image 1), look at the area around x=510-552. Is there a visible thin red wick line that extends below the broad red fill into a separate region? Or does the red simply end at the bottom of the broad fill?\\n\\n4. Compare the disputed region (x≈510-552, y≈833-840) with the nearby green candle at x≈577 (y_end=740). Does the green candle show a similar pattern of thin trace through broad fill? Is the green one clearly a normal wick-over-body?\\n\\n5. For the red candidates at x=510 and x=552: is the \\"thin trace\\" actually just the EDGE of the broad fill (i.e., the fill's left/right boundary appearing as a thin vertical line in the crop), or is it a genuinely separate 1-2px line running through the interior of the fill?\\n\\nBe precise and specific. Do not assume a hidden wick exists. Report what you actually see in the pixels."}	{"ok": true, "tool": "vision_image", "paths": ["C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\norm images\\\\QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916.jpg", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\occlusion_crop_01_zoom4x.png", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\occlusion_crop_02_zoom4x.png", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\occlusion_crop_10_zoom4x.png", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\focus_scan_01_8x.png", "C:\\\\Users\\\\KHzz\\\\Documents\\\\Norm\\\\images\\\\analysis\\\\20260917-040740-f9381676-QQQ_followup_hidden_wick_test_20260916-high\\\\focus_scan_02_8x.png"], "answer": "I examined the original (image 1) and the five zoomed crops (images 2–6) pixel‑by‑pixel in the disputed column (x≈510–552). Here is exactly what the pixels show, per your questions.\\n\\n**1. Thin trace below the broad red fill? No.**\\nIn every crop that contains the disputed red bar (images 2, 3, 5, 6), the only thin vertical red trace (≈1–3 px) is at the **top** of the broad red fill, running **upward** into the lavender price‑pane background. The bottom edge of the broad red fill is the lowest red pixel in that column — it terminates flat on the volume pane's 0 baseline. There is **no** separate thin red line continuing below the fill. The interior of the fill is uniform solid red with no internal vertical line.\\n\\n**2. Candle body or volume bar? Volume bar.**\\nThe broad red region is a **volume bar**, not a price candle body. Evidence:\\n- It sits in the middle pane (the one with the `0` / `100` axis on the right), i.e. the volume pane, not the 700–712 price pane.\\n- It is a wide rectangle (one bar period) resting on the 0 baseline, in the same horizontal band as the adjacent green bars.\\n- Your own axis mapping confirms this: y≈833–840 maps to ~697.8–698.0, which is *below* the 700 gridline (y≈740) — i.e. outside the price pane and inside the volume pane. A price candle body cannot live there.\\n- The genuine price candle bodies are higher up (≈y 400–620), narrower, and carry the wicks.\\n\\n**3. Thin red wick below the fill in the original? No.**\\nIn image 1 at x≈510–552 the red simply ends at the bottom of the broad fill (the 0 line, ≈y 870). The thin red wick is at the **top**, extending up into the price pane. Below the fill is the oscillator pane (its own separate red histogram), not a continuation of this bar's wick. No thin red line bridges from the bottom of the fill downward.\\n\\n**4. Comparison with the green bar at x≈577.**\\nThe green shows the **same** structure, not a different one: a thin green vertical line at the **top** (the price‑pane wick) stacked above a broad green fill (the volume bar). It is a normal wick‑over‑bar vertical alignment, with the thin trace at the top. It does **not** show a thin trace below the fill either. So the red and green are consistent: thin trace = top wick; broad fill = volume bar.\\n\\n**5. Edge of the fill vs. separate interior line? Neither — it's the price‑pane wick.**\\nThe thin vertical red line is **not** the left/right boundary of the fill (those are horizontal‑top/bottom edges, and the fill's left/right sides are vertical *edges of the rectangle*, not a 1–2 px line), and it is **not** a 1–2 px line running through the interior of the fill (the interior is solid). It is the **candle wick from the price pane above**, a separate element that, in the 2‑D crop, appears stacked directly on top of the volume bar because both share the same x‑column.\\n\\n**Synthesis.** The \\"deep red area\\" you are auditing is a **volume bar** in the volume pane. The thin red trace that is the subject of the dispute is the **price‑pane candle wick** sitting *above* that bar. There is no hidden lower wick, no thin trace through the fill's interior, and no red pixel below the fill's bottom edge. The red ends at the 0 baseline; the only thin red line is the upward wick in the pane above.", "image_count": 6}	f	2026-09-17 04:10:29.760543-04
