184	1725	chat-9dcf1661-503d-47f8-8490-095c43ffdbf2	step-01	read_file	{"path": "D:\\\\LOCAL_Share\\\\Code Projects\\\\ca8d-tailnet-host\\\\.env.example"}	{"ok": true, "path": "D:\\\\LOCAL_Share\\\\Code Projects\\\\ca8d-tailnet-host\\\\.env.example", "size": 6198, "tool": "read_file", "sha256": "c1eeab6ff57fc5853af7230c2e2602e7b0b19dbe7f71529134726812e7750b5f", "content": "# =============================================================================\\n# ca8d-tailnet-host — environment configuration\\n# =============================================================================\\n# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values on the ca8d QNAP NAS.\\n#   cp .env.example .env\\n#\\n# IMPORTANT:\\n#  - `.env` is git-ignored. Never commit real secrets.\\n#  - All host bind-mount paths are configurable below. Do NOT hard-code a\\n#    specific QNAP share/volume name (e.g. /share/Container) in the compose\\n#    file — always reference these variables so the project stays portable.\\n#  - All paths should be absolute POSIX paths valid on the QNAP (Linux) host.\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 1. Host bind-mount base directory\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Base directory on the QNAP host where all durable state lives.\\n# Choose a location on a share you control. Examples (pick ONE, do not hard-code):\\n#   /share/CACHEDEV1_DATA/ca8d-tailnet-host\\n#   /share/Container/ca8d-tailnet-host\\n#   /volume1/ca8d-tailnet-host\\n#\\n# All sub-paths below are derived from this base unless overridden individually.\\nHOST_DATA_BASE=/share/CACHEDEV1_DATA/ca8d-tailnet-host\\n\\n# Individual durable-state directories (defaults derive from HOST_DATA_BASE).\\n# Override any of these if you want a specific state dir on a different share.\\nPIHOLE_DATA_DIR=${HOST_DATA_BASE}/pihole\\nTRAEFIK_DATA_DIR=${HOST_DATA_BASE}/traefik\\nDEVBOX_HOME_DIR=${HOST_DATA_BASE}/devbox-home\\nSSH_AUTHORIZED_KEYS_DIR=${HOST_DATA_BASE}/ssh\\nTLS_CERTS_DIR=${HOST_DATA_BASE}/certs\\nBACKUP_DIR=${HOST_DATA_BASE}/backups\\n\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 2. Tailscale identity\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# The ca8d MagicDNS / Tailscale FQDN for this host, e.g. ca8d.tailnet-name.ts.net\\n# Used for Traefik routers, TLS SANs, and documentation. No public domain needed.\\nTAILSCALE_FQDN=ca8d.example.ts.net\\n\\n# Optional: the Tailscale interface name (usually tailscale0). Used only for\\n# documentation and optional bind-target selection. Leave as-is unless unusual.\\nTAILSCALE_IFACE=tailscale0\\n\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 3. TLS / HTTPS mode\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# TLS_MODE selects how HTTPS is provided inside the tailnet:\\n#   tailscale  — Preferred. Use Tailscale-issued certs (tailscale cert) for the\\n#                TAILSCALE_FQDN. Requires the host to run Tailscale with\\n#                HTTPS certs enabled and the FQDN to be a MagicDNS name.\\n#   internal-ca — Fallback. Use a self-signed / internal-CA cert you generate\\n#                and place in TLS_CERTS_DIR. Documented in the README.\\n#   none       — HTTP only (no TLS). For quick local testing; not recommended.\\nTLS_MODE=tailscale\\n\\n# When TLS_MODE=internal-ca, the cert/key files to load (paths under TLS_CERTS_DIR\\n# or absolute). Leave blank when using TLS_MODE=tailscale.\\nTLS_CERT_FILE=\\nTLS_KEY_FILE=\\n\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 4. Traefik\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Host ports Traefik listens on. Bound to the Tailscale interface / loopback\\n# only (see compose file) — NOT 0.0.0.0 — so they are tailnet-private.\\nTRAEFIK_HTTP_PORT=8080\\nTRAEFIK_HTTPS_PORT=8443\\n\\n# Traefik dashboard (optional, tailnet-only). Set to \\"true\\" to enable.\\nTRAEFIK_DASHBOARD_ENABLED=false\\nTRAEFIK_DASHBOARD_PORT=8081\\n\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 5. Pi-hole\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# DNS port. Default 53. QNAP may already use 53 (e.g. its own DNS service or\\n# another container). If 53 is taken, change this to a free port (e.g. 5353)\\n# and point tailnet clients at that port. Run scripts/detect-port-conflicts.sh\\n# to check before starting.\\nPIHOLE_DNS_PORT=53\\n\\n# Pi-hole admin UI is served on container port 80 and routed through Traefik\\n# (see TAILSCALE_FQDN + router rules). No direct host port is required.\\n# Set PIHOLE_ADMIN_PORT only if you want a direct host port as a fallback.\\n# Leave blank to rely on Traefik routing.\\nPIHOLE_ADMIN_PORT=\\n\\n# REQUIRED: Pi-hole admin password. Generate a strong value, e.g.:\\n#   openssl rand -base64 24\\nPIHOLE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_STRONG_PASSWORD\\n\\n# Pi-hole upstream DNS (what it forwards to). Defaults to Tailscale's DNS\\n# (100.100.100.100) so tailnet names resolve. Change if you prefer another.\\nPIHOLE_UPSTREAM_DNS=100.100.100.100\\n\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 6. Devbox (persistent Linux dev container)\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# SSH port on the host. Nonstandard by default to reduce scan noise.\\n# Bound to the Tailscale interface / loopback only (see compose file).\\nDEVBOX_SSH_PORT=2222\\n\\n# Devbox login user (non-root). SSH requires key-based auth (see\\n# SSH_AUTHORIZED_KEYS_DIR). No anonymous access.\\nDEVBOX_USER=dev\\nDEVBOX_UID=1000\\nDEVBOX_GID=1000\\n\\n# Working directory inside the devbox where projects live (bind-mounted).\\nDEVBOX_WORKSPACE_DIR=${HOST_DATA_BASE}/devbox-home/workspace\\n\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 7. Test HTTP service\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Minimal HTTP service behind Traefik to prove routing independently of Pi-hole.\\n# Served on container port 8080, routed via Traefik. No direct host port needed.\\nTEST_HTTP_PORT=8080\\n\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 8. Misc\\n# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Container timezone (IANA). Affects logs and timestamps.\\nTZ=America/New_York\\n\\n# Compose project name (used as a prefix for networks/containers).\\nCOMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=ca8d-tailnet-host\\n", "end_line": 129, "start_line": 1, "total_lines": 129}	f	2026-09-20 12:08:22.864396-04	2026-09-20 12:28:54.334864-04
185	1729	chat-9dcf1661-503d-47f8-8490-095c43ffdbf2	step-01	read_file	{"path": "D:\\\\LOCAL_Share\\\\Code Projects\\\\norm_step03_result.md"}	{"ok": true, "path": "D:\\\\LOCAL_Share\\\\Code Projects\\\\norm_step03_result.md", "size": 5768, "tool": "read_file", "sha256": "e87e07b5b7b6965209ae8c2ffa9aeaa6ab8f51a7a6ebea6b62db9965b925842c", "content": "## Step 3 Result: Candidate Discard Evaluation\\n\\n**14-tile hand:** 4m, 6m, 7m, 3s, 4s, 7s, 9s, 3p, 4p, 6p, 7p, E, S, N\\n**Drawn tile:** 6m (creates new 6-7m ryanmen)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Shanten Verification (All Discards)\\n\\n**Key finding: Every possible discard from this hand results in 4-shanten.** No single discard can reduce the hand below 4-shanten, and no discard worsens it to 5-shanten. The differentiator is purely **ukeire** (tile acceptance).\\n\\n**Why:** The hand has 5 taatsu (67m, 34s, 79s, 34p, 67p) and 0 pairs. Discarding any tile either:\\n- Removes an isolated tile → keeps all 5 taatsu (best ukeire)\\n- Breaks one taatsu → 4 taatsu remain (or 5 if a new taatsu forms from remaining tiles)\\n- Cannot break two taatsu simultaneously\\n\\nShanten formula: 8 − 2×(melds) − min(taatsu, 4) − (pair? 1:0) = 8 − 0 − 4 − 0 = **4** in all cases.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Comparison Table (Top Candidates by Ukeire)\\n\\n| Discard | Shanten | Ukeire | Ukeire Breakdown | Risk / Notes |\\n|---------|:-------:|:------:|------------------|--------------|\\n| **South (S)** | 4 | **71** | 32 completing + 39 pair | Safest (honor). Least valuable wind (1000-pt yakuhai only). Lowest cost to discard. |\\n| **East (E)** | 4 | **71** | 32 completing + 39 pair | Safe (honor). Round wind (2000-pt). Slightly more likely to be targeted by opponents. |\\n| **North (N)** | 4 | **71** | 32 completing + 39 pair | Safe (honor). Our seat wind (2000-pt). Slightly more useful to keep than S. |\\n| **4-man** | 4 | **71** | 32 completing + 39 pair | Slightly more dangerous (number tile, could fill a seq wait). Retains all 5 taatsu. |\\n| 7-man | 4 | **67** | 28 completing + 39 pair | 67m→46m kanchan (loses 8m). Still 5 taatsu. More dangerous (number tile). |\\n| 9-sou | 4 | **67** | 28 completing + 39 pair | Breaks 79s kanchan (loses 8s). 4 taatsu remain. Moderate danger. |\\n| 7-sou | 4 | **67** | 28 completing + 39 pair | Breaks 79s kanchan (loses 8s). 4 taatsu remain. Moderate danger. |\\n| 3-pin | 4 | **67** | 28 completing + 39 pair | 34p→46p kanchan (loses 2p). Still 5 taatsu. Moderate danger. |\\n| 7-pin | 4 | **67** | 28 completing + 39 pair | 67p→46p kanchan (loses 8p). Still 5 taatsu. Moderate danger. |\\n| 6-man | 4 | **63** | 24 completing + 39 pair | Breaks 67m, no new taatsu (4m-7m gap too wide). 4 taatsu. Undoes the draw. |\\n| 3-sou | 4 | **63** | 24 completing + 39 pair | Breaks 34s, no new taatsu (4s-7s gap too wide). 4 taatsu. |\\n| 4-sou | 4 | **63** | 24 completing + 39 pair | Breaks 34s, no new taatsu (3s-7s gap too wide). 4 taatsu. |\\n| 4-pin | 4 | **63** | 24 completing + 39 pair | Breaks 34p, no new taatsu (3p-6p gap too wide). 4 taatsu. |\\n| 6-pin | 4 | **63** | 24 completing + 39 pair | Breaks 67p, no new taatsu (4p-7p gap too wide). 4 taatsu. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Ukeire Detail (Top Tier: E, S, N, 4m — all 71)\\n\\n**Taatsu-completing tiles (8 types × 4 copies = 32):**\\n5m, 8m (complete 67m) · 2s, 5s (complete 34s) · 8s (complete 79s) · 2p, 5p (complete 34p) · 5p, 8p (complete 67p)\\n\\n**Pair-forming tiles (13 types × 3 copies = 39):**\\nAny of the 13 remaining tiles in hand (each has 3 copies left in the wall)\\n\\n**Total: 71 tiles reduce shanten from 4 → 3.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Ukeire Detail (Second Tier: 7m, 9s, 7s, 3p, 7p — all 67)\\n\\nThese lose one completing type (4 copies) compared to the top tier:\\n- 7m: loses 8m (67m becomes 46m kanchan)\\n- 9s/7s: loses 8s (79s broken)\\n- 3p: loses 2p (34p becomes 46p kanchan)\\n- 7p: loses 8p (67p becomes 46p kanchan)\\n\\n**Total: 67 tiles reduce shanten from 4 → 3.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Ukeire Detail (Third Tier: 6m, 3s, 4s, 4p, 6p — all 63)\\n\\nThese lose two completing types (8 copies) compared to the top tier:\\n- 6m: loses 5m and 8m (67m broken, no replacement)\\n- 3s/4s: loses 2s and 5s (34s broken, no replacement)\\n- 4p: loses 2p and 5p (34p broken, no replacement)\\n- 6p: loses 5p and 8p (67p broken, no replacement)\\n\\n**Total: 63 tiles reduce shanten from 4 → 3.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Key Observations for Step 4/5\\n\\n1. **All discards are 4-shanten.** The hand is far from tenpai regardless of choice. The decision is about maximizing ukeire and minimizing risk.\\n\\n2. **Top tier (E, S, N, 4m) are structurally identical** in shanten and ukeire (71). The choice among them is driven by safety and tile value.\\n\\n3. **Honor tiles are safest to discard** (can't fill a sequence wait). Among honors:\\n   - **South** is the least valuable to retain (only 1000-pt yakuhai, vs. 2000 for E/N)\\n   - **East** is the round wind (slightly more likely to be in opponents' hands)\\n   - **North** is our seat wind (2000-pt value if paired)\\n\\n4. **4-man is the only number-tile option** in the top tier. Marginally more dangerous to discard (could fill a ryanmen/kanchan wait) but retains maximum offensive flexibility.\\n\\n5. **No immediate defensive pressure** at 4-shanten in East 3. The hand is too far from completion for aggressive defense to be necessary. Offense (maximizing ukeire) is the correct posture.\\n\\n6. **Recommended shortlist for step 4/5:** South (S) is the most natural beginner-friendly discard — safest, least valuable to keep, and structurally identical to the other top options. East and North are equally valid alternatives.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Durable Facts for Subsequent Steps\\n\\n- **All discards: 4-shanten** (no discard changes shanten)\\n- **Top tier (71 ukeire):** E, S, N, 4m\\n- **Second tier (67 ukeire):** 7m, 9s, 7s, 3p, 7p\\n- **Third tier (63 ukeire):** 6m, 3s, 4s, 4p, 6p\\n- Ukeire composition (top tier): 32 taatsu-completing + 39 pair-forming\\n- Safety ranking (safest → riskiest): S ≈ N > E > 4m > number tiles\\n- Value ranking (most → least useful to keep): E ≈ N > S > 4m\\n- No immediate defensive pressure at 4-shanten in East 3\\n- Offense (maximize ukeire) is the correct posture\\n\\n*This is an intermediate step result, not the final answer.*\\n", "end_line": 109, "start_line": 1, "total_lines": 109}	f	2026-09-20 12:11:55.854631-04	2026-09-20 12:28:54.334864-04
186	1730	chat-9dcf1661-503d-47f8-8490-095c43ffdbf2	step-01	read_file	{"path": "D:\\\\LOCAL_Share\\\\Code Projects\\\\norm_step04_result.md"}	{"ok": true, "path": "D:\\\\LOCAL_Share\\\\Code Projects\\\\norm_step04_result.md", "size": 4508, "tool": "read_file", "sha256": "5de1685234eb299b97b0035655a63ef7d89cf53ef6a269469e4f12d8550f3408", "content": "## Step 4 Result: Table Context and Safety Assessment\\n\\n**Round:** East 3 (3rd hand of the East block)\\n**Seat:** North (bottom player, last to act)\\n**Hand state:** 4-shanten, 0 completed melds, 5 taatsu, 0 pairs\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Round Position Analysis (East 3)\\n\\n- **Mid-round, not late.** East 3 is the third of four East-block hands. There is still one full hand (East 4) remaining after this one, so the round is not in its final stretch.\\n- **No urgency to force a win.** At 4-shanten, the hand needs roughly 4 more useful draws to reach tenpai. In East 3, that is achievable but not guaranteed. There is no \\"last-chance\\" pressure.\\n- **Opponents have had 2 prior hands** to build. They may be 1–2 shanten ahead of us. This means the risk of dealing into a strong hand is moderate but not extreme.\\n- **Dealer is still East.** The dealer (East seat) has higher stakes (13,000 vs. 9,000 for non-dealer wins/losses in a non-riichi hand). As North (non-dealer), our downside is slightly lower, which supports a measured offensive posture rather than aggressive defense.\\n\\n### Seat Analysis (North)\\n\\n- **Last to act.** North discards after East, South, and West. We see all three opponents' discards before committing, giving us the most information at the table. This is a structural advantage for both offense (reading what they need) and defense (avoiding their waits).\\n- **Seat wind = North.** The North tile in our hand is our personal seat wind (2,000-pt yakuhai if paired). This makes it slightly more valuable to retain than South.\\n- **Round wind = East.** The East tile is the round wind for everyone (2,000-pt yakuhai). Opponents are more likely to be targeting East for a yakuhai hand, making it marginally more dangerous to discard than South.\\n- **South is neither our seat wind nor the round wind.** It carries only 1,000-pt yakuhai value. It is the least \\"targeted\\" honor in the hand.\\n\\n### Safety Ranking of Top-Tier Discards (all 71 ukeire)\\n\\n| Discard | Safety | Reasoning |\\n|---------|--------|-----------|\\n| **South** | **Safest** | Honor tile (can't fill a sequence wait). Not round wind, not our seat wind. Only 1,000-pt yakuhai. Least likely to be a key tile in an opponent's hand. |\\n| **North** | Safe | Honor tile. Our seat wind (2,000-pt), but opponents are less likely to be specifically targeting it. Slightly more useful to keep than South. |\\n| **East** | Moderately safe | Honor tile. Round wind (2,000-pt) — all opponents may be targeting it for yakuhai. Slightly higher deal-in risk than South or North. |\\n| **4-man** | Most dangerous of top tier | Number tile. Middle tile (3–5m ryanmen, 2–6m kanchan, 5–6m ryanmen all possible waits it could fill). Highest deal-in risk among the four. |\\n\\n### Offense vs. Defense Posture\\n\\n**Verdict: Offense (build the hand) is the correct posture.**\\n\\nJustification:\\n1. **4-shanten is far from tenpai.** There is no scenario where this hand is an immediate threat to opponents, so aggressive defense (folding, discarding only perfectly safe tiles) is unnecessary and would sacrifice ukeire for no gain.\\n2. **No riichi or strong-hand signal is indicated.** The problem statement provides no evidence of an opponent declaring riichi or showing a clearly dangerous hand. In the absence of such signals, the default is to build.\\n3. **East 3 is not late enough to force defense.** In East 4 (the final hand), a 4-shanten hand would be a candidate for folding. In East 3, with a full hand still remaining, continuing to build is standard.\\n4. **All top-tier discards have identical ukeire (71).** Since the offensive value is the same regardless of which top-tier tile we discard, the tiebreaker is safety. This means we can choose the safest option (South) with zero offensive cost.\\n\\n### Durable Facts for Step 5\\n\\n- **Posture: Offense.** Build the hand; no defensive folding is warranted.\\n- **Safety hierarchy (top tier):** South (safest) > North > East > 4-man (riskiest).\\n- **Round context:** East 3, mid-round, no urgency, no visible danger.\\n- **Seat context:** North (last to act, information advantage). North tile is our seat wind (slightly more valuable to keep). East is round wind (slightly more dangerous to discard).\\n- **Key conclusion:** Among the four structurally identical top-tier discards (E, S, N, 4m), **South is the optimal choice** — it is the safest to discard, the least valuable to retain, and costs zero ukeire. This is the natural recommendation for a beginner.\\n\\n*This is an intermediate step result, not the final answer.*\\n", "end_line": 50, "start_line": 1, "total_lines": 50}	f	2026-09-20 12:11:55.889949-04	2026-09-20 12:28:54.334864-04
