 2024 |\n| `s.json` | Array of `{"month","day","year","type":"circle"}` objects | 182 | 2024 |\n| `test.json` | Array of `{"month","day","year","type":"circle"}` objects | 92 | 2024 |\n\n`s.json` is the converted union of `1.json` + `2.json` (52 + 130 = 182), confirming the merge workflow.\n\n## Current State / Completeness\n\n**Observed facts:**\n\n- Both scripts are syntactically complete and runnable as standalone GUI applications.\n- No dependency manifest (`requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `setup.py`) is present.\n- No tests, CI configuration, or package structure exist.\n- No `README.md` existed prior to this file.\n- The `.idea/` directory indicates the project was opened in a JetBrains IDE (likely PyCharm).\n\n**Known limitation (observed in source):**\n\n- `main.py` writes already-converted `{"month","day","year","type"}` objects to JSON, while `fuser.py`'s `add_to_merged_data` expects a **flat list of `"yyyy-MM-dd"` strings** and performs the conversion itself. Feeding `main.py`'s output directly into `fuser.py` would cause a runtime error. The two scripts therefore operate on **different input schemas** and are not directly composable without an intermediate conversion step.\n\n**Inference:**\n\n- The `"type": "circle"` field is constant across every output object; its semantic meaning (likely a calendar-rendering hint for a downstream consumer) is not documented in any source file.\n- The project appears to be a personal or prototype tool rather than a library or product — no versioning, no public API, no documentation beyond this README.\n```\n\n---\n\n### Durable result for step 6 (Write README.md)\n\n- **Target path:** `D:\\LOCAL_Share\\Code Projects\\jsonWeeksGenerator\\README.md`\n- **Content:** The markdown block above (between the triple-backtick fences).\n- **All four required sections present:** Primary Language ✓, Probable Purpose ✓, Project Structure ✓, Current State/Completeness ✓\n- **Fact vs. inference separation:** Inferences are expli