header is **90% transparent**. The site's primary identity section is invisible without JavaScript. This is a progressive-enhancement failure.\n- **Cosmetic:** Double semicolon `;;`.\n\n---\n\n#### Behavior 2: One-Time Viewport Check (lines 152–158)\n```js\nif ($(window).width() > 1500) {\n    $('#s2').click()\n} else {\n    $('.accordion-child + .menu-item').addClass('small-version');\n    $('#main-content').addClass('small-version')\n    $('#navbar').addClass('small-version')\n}\n```\n- **Purpose:** On wide screens, programmatically opens the nav (clicks the checkbox). On narrow screens, adds `.small-version` class to constrain layout.\n- **Brittle — no resize handler:** This runs **once** on page load. If the user resizes the browser, crosses the 1500px threshold, or rotates a tablet, nothing updates. There is no `window.addEventListener('resize', ...)`.\n- **Brittle — selector coupling:** `$('.accordion-child + .menu-item')` is a CSS sibling combinator used as a jQuery selector. It works because all nav buttons share the `.accordion-child` class, but it's fragile to markup changes.\n- **Brittle — programmatic click:** `$('#s2').click()` triggers the checkbox's `.click` handler (Behavior 5), coupling the "open nav on wide screens" logic to the toggle handler. If the toggle handler changes, this breaks silently.\n\n---\n\n#### Behavior 3: Manual Sticky Nav (lines 159–162)\n```js\ndocument.onscroll = function () {\n    var offset = $(document).scrollTop();\n    $('#navbar').css('top', offset);\n}\n```\n- **Purpose:** Makes `#navbar` stick to the top of the viewport as the user scrolls. The CSS sets `position: absolute; top: 10%`; the JS overrides `top` to the scroll offset on every scroll event.\n- **Brittle — `document.onscroll` assignment:** This **replaces** any existing scroll handler. If Bootstrap, AOS, or any other library sets `document.onscroll`, it gets overwritten. Should use `addEventListener('scroll', ..., { passive: true })`.\n- **Brittle — no throttling:** Fires on every scroll event with no `requestAnimationFrame` or debounce. On mobile, this causes layout thrashing (read `scrollTop`, write `top` CSS, trigger reflow).\n- **Brittle — no `passive: true`:** The browser cannot optimize scrolling because it must wait for the handler to complete.\n- **Simplifiable:** This entire behavior can be replaced with **one CSS property**: `#navbar { position: sticky; top: 0; }`. No JS needed.\n\n---\n\n#### Behavior 4: Finance Button Hover Highlight (lines 164–175)\n```js\nfunction indicator(e) {\n    marker.css('left', e.offsetLeft);\n    marker.css('width', e.offsetWidth);\n}\nitem.forEach(link => {\n    link.addEventListener("mouseover", (e) => {\n        indicator(e.target);\n    })\n})\n```\n- **Purpose:** Moves a green `#highlight-box` div to align under the hovered finance button. Purely decorative.\n- **Brittle — `mouseover` not `mouseenter`:** `mouseover` fires when the pointer moves between child elements of the button, causing redundant repositioning. `mouseenter` would be correct.\n- **Brittle — no `mouseleave`:** The highlight stays on the last hovered button permanently. There is no handler to hide or reset it.\n- **Brittle — no touch support:** No `touchstart`/`touchend` handlers. On mobile, the highlight never appears.\n- **Brittle — `offsetLeft`/`offsetWidth`:** These are relative to the element's `offsetParent`, which may not be `#finance-button-box`. If the DOM structure changes, the positioning silently breaks.\n- **Brittle — unit mixing:** CSS sets `#highlight-box { width: 15% }` (percentage of parent). JS overrides with `offsetWidth` (pixels). The initial percentage is meaningless once JS runs, but if JS fails, the box is 15% wide and mispositioned.\n- **Redundant:** The buttons already have Bootstrap `btn-outline-light` with built-in hover states. This custom highlight adds marginal visual value for significant fragility.\n- **Simplifiable:** Could be replaced with a CSS `:hover` pseudo-element on each button, or removed entirely.\n\n---\n\n#### Behavior 5: Nav Toggle Handler (lines 177–184)\n```js\n$('#s2').click(function () {\n    $('.left-offsetter').toggleClass('blue-back');\n    $('#ham-icon').toggleClass('open')\n    if ($('#s2').is(':checked')) {\n        $('#nav-area').addClass('hazy-black');\n    } else $('#nav-area').removeClass('hazy-black');\n})\n```\n- **Purpose:** Toggles the hamburger icon to an X, adds/removes the red skewed overlay (`.hazy-black`), and toggles `.blue-back` on `.left-offsetter`.\n- **Dead code:** `$('.left-offsetter')` — **no element in the HTML has class `.left-offsetter`**. This line does nothing.\n- **Split-brain design:** The checkbox `#s2` drives the menu open/close via **CSS** (`input:checked ~ .accordion-child`). The JS only handles the hamburger animation and the hazy-black overlay. The visual state is split between CSS and JS, making it hard to reason about.\n- **Registration outside `$(document).ready`:** This handler is registered at the top level of the script, not inside the `ready` callback. It works because the script is at the bottom of `<body>`, but it's inconsistent with the pattern used above and would break if the script were moved to `<head>`.\n- **Coupled to Behavior 2:** The `$('#s2').click()` call in the viewport check triggers this handler. Changing the toggle logic affects the "open on wide screens" behavior.\n\n---\n\n#### Behavior 6: Resume Content Loading (lines 186–188)\n```js\n$('#div-resume').load('/files/resume.html', function () {\n    $("#main-content").css('min-height', $('#div-resume').height() + this.height());\n})\n```\n- **Purpose:** Loads `resume.html` into `#div-resume` via jQuery's `.load()` (an AJAX wrapper).\n- **Broken reference:** `resume.html` contains `<script src="/js/resumeSection.js"></script>`. jQuery's `.load()` executes this script. **The file does not exist** — this throws a 404 in the console on every page load.\n- **Global style injection:** `resume.html` contains a `<style>