ior is a UX choice, not a layout necessity. |\n| **150** | `$('.accordion-child + .menu-item').addClass('small-version');` | Adds `.small-version` class to menu items. **Purely visual** — the CSS rules for `.small-version` (LESS L57, L107, L175, L210) control sizing. Should be a CSS media query. |\n| **151** | `$('#main-content').addClass('small-version')` | Adds `.small-version` to main content. **Purely visual** — controls `top: 400px` offset (LESS L57). Should be a CSS media query. |\n| **152** | `$('#navbar').addClass('small-version')` | Adds `.small-version` to navbar. **Purely visual** — controls nav sizing (LESS L210). Should be a CSS media query. |\n\n**Recommendation:** **REPLACE with CSS.** Remove the entire `if/else` block (lines 147–153). Add a CSS media query in the LESS file:\n```less\n@media (max-width: 1500px) {\n  .accordion-child + .menu-item { /* small-version styles */ }\n  #main-content { /* small-version styles */ }\n  #navbar { /* small-version styles */ }\n}\n```\nThe `$('#s2').click()` auto-open on desktop can be preserved as a one-shot behavioral action (not viewport-dependent layout) or removed if the menu should start closed on all sizes.\n\n**Mobile issue at 360px:** The `else` branch fires, adding `.small-version` which sets `#main-content { top: 400px }` — pushing content 400px down on a phone. This is a **layout bug** caused by JS.\n\n**Layout thrashing risk:** Low (one-shot at load), but the class toggling forces a reflow of the entire page.\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Scroll Handler — **KEEP (behavioral), but flag for mobile**\n\n| Line | Code | Issue |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **154** | `document.onscroll = function () {` | **ISSUE.** Uses `document.onscroll` (overwrites any other scroll handler). Fires on every scroll frame. |\n| **155** | `var offset = $(document).scrollTop();` | Reads scroll position. |\n| **156** | `$('#navbar').css('top', offset);` | **ISSUE.** Sets `top` to the scroll offset, making the navbar scroll with the page. This is a **behavioral** action (sticky/fixed positioning) that CSS can do natively with `position: sticky` or `position: fixed`. |\n\n**Recommendation:** **MODIFY.** Replace with CSS `position: sticky; top: 0;` on `#navbar`. This eliminates the JS scroll handler entirely, removes the per-frame DOM write (layout thrashing), and works identically on all screen sizes. The JS scroll handler is a **layout thrashing risk** on mobile because it fires on every scroll event and writes a CSS property, forcing a reflow each frame.\n\n**Mobile issue:** On a 360px phone, the navbar (with `min-width: 400px` from LESS) is already wider than the viewport. The scroll handler doesn't fix this but doesn't make it worse. The real issue is the `min-width` in CSS.\n\n**Performance:** `document.onscroll` + `$(document).scrollTop()` + `.css('top', ...)` on every scroll frame is a **layout thrashing** pattern (read scroll position → write CSS → browser reflows). On mobile with touch scrolling, this fires very frequently.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Finance Button Hover Indicator — **KEEP (behavioral), minor mobile note**\n\n| Line | Code | Issue |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **144** | `var marker = $('#highlight-box');` | Selects the highlight box element. |\n| **145** | `var item = document.querySelectorAll('.finance-button-item');` | Selects all finance buttons. |\n| **159** | `function indicator(e) {` | Positions the highlight marker. |\n| **160** | `marker.css('left', e.offsetLeft);` | **ISSUE.** Sets `left` based on `offsetLeft`. On mobile, `mouseover` does not fire (touch devices use `touchstart`/`click`). This code is **dead on mobile** — no harm, no benefit. |\n| **161** | `marker.css('width', e.offsetWidth);` | Same as above. |\n| **164–168** | `item.forEach(link => { link.addEventListener("mouseover", ...) })` | **ISSUE.** `mouseover` is a mouse-only event. On touch devices, this never fires. Not a bug, but dead code on mobile. |\n\n**Recommendation:** **KEEP AS-IS.** This is a hover effect for desktop. It's dead code on mobile (no harm). If desired, could add `touchstart`/`click` handlers for mobile, but that's out of scope for responsive fixes. No viewport-size dependency. No layout thrashing (only fires on mouseover, not on scroll/resize).\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Nav Toggle (Hamburger) Logic — **KEEP (behavioral)**\n\n| Line | Code | Issue |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **170** | `$('#s2').click(function () {` | Toggles the nav checkbox on click. |\n| **171** | `$('.left-offsetter').toggleClass('blue-back');` | Toggles a class. **Note:** `.left-offsetter` does not appear in the HTML — this is a **dead selector** (no matching element). No effect. |\n| **172** | `$('#ham-icon').toggleClass('open')` | Toggles the hamburger icon animation. **Behavioral** — CSS cannot do this (it's a state toggle). |\n| **173–175** | `if ($('#s2').is(':checked')) { ... } else { ... }` | Toggles `.hazy-black` on `#nav-area`. **Behavioral** — shows/hides the nav backdrop. |\n\n**Recommendation:** **KEEP AS-IS.** This is genuine interactive behavior (toggle state) that CSS cannot replicate. No viewport-size dependency. No layout thrashing. The dead `.left-offsetter` selector is harmless (no matching element).\n\n**Mobile note:** The hamburger icon (`#ham-icon`) has `width: 60px; height: 45px` (LESS L224, L228) — adequate touch target (≥44px). The `role="button"` on a `<div>` is an accessibility concern but not a responsive issue.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Resume Section Load + Height Adjustment — **MODIFY**\n\n| Line | Code | Issue |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **179** | `$('#div-resume').load('/files/resume.html', function () {` | Loads the resume fragment via jQuery `.load()`. **Behavioral** — dynamic content loading. |\n| **180** | `$("#main-content").css('min-height', $('#div-resume').height() + this.height());` | **ISSUE.** Reads `$('#div-resume').height()` (forces layout/reflow) and sets `min-height` on `#main-content`. This is a **layout thrashing** pattern: read height → write min-height → browser reflows. |\n\n**Recommendation:** **MODIFY.** The `min-height` adjustment is a **visual concern** (ensuring the main content area is tall enough). This can be handled by:\n1. Removing the JS height calculation entirely (the content will naturally flow), OR\n2. Using CSS `min-height: 100vh` (already set inline on `#main-content`) and letting the content grow naturally.\n\nThe `#main-content` already has `style="min-height: 100vh"` inline. The JS is trying to make it *taller* than 100vh based on the resume content height. This is unnecessary — the content will naturally push the container taller. **Remove the height calculation.**\n\n**Mobile issue:** On a 360px phone, the resume content (with `width: 125%` from `resume.html`) overflows. The JS height calculation doesn't fix this. The `min-height` write forces a reflow on mobile, which is expensive.\n\n**Broken reference:** The loaded `resume.html` references `/js/resumeSection.js` which **does not exist**. This will produce a 404 error in the console but no functional impact (the script tag simply fails to load).\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Head Class Addition — **KEEP (harmless)**\n\n| Line | Code | Issue |\n|------|------|-------|\n| **146** | `$('#s-head').addClass('on-load');;` | Adds `.on-load` class. **Note:** Double semicolon (typo, harmless). The `.on-load` class is not defined in the LESS file — this is a **dead class** with no CSS effect. |\n\n**Recommendation:** **KEEP AS-IS** (or remove as dead code, but out of scope). No viewport dependency. No mobile issue.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table: All Viewport-Dependent / Layout-Affecting JS\n\n| # | File | Line(s) | Code Pattern | Type | Mobile Issue | Recommendation |\n|---|------|---------|-------------|------|-------------|----------------|\n| 1 | `index.html` | 147–153 | `$(window).width() > 1500` → toggle `.small-version` classes | **Viewport detection** | Adds `top: 400px` to content on mobile; one-shot (no resize handling) | **REPLACE with CSS media query** |\n| 2 | `index.html` | 148 | `$('#s2').click()` (auto-open nav on desktop) | **Behavioral** (tied to viewport check) | N/A (desktop-only action) | **KEEP** as one-shot behavioral action, decouple from viewport check |\n| 3 | `index.html` | 154–157 | `document.onscroll` → `$('#navbar').css('top', offset)` | **Scroll-driven layout** | Layout thrashing on every scroll frame; navbar already has `min-width: 400px` | **REPLACE with CSS `position: sticky`** |\n| 4 | `index.html` | 159–168 | `mouseover` → `marker.css('left/width', ...)` | **Hover effect** | Dead on mobile (no mouseover); no harm | **KEEP AS-IS** |\n| 5 | `index.html` | 170–176 | `$('#s2').click()` → toggle nav state | **Behavioral** | None (touch-friendly, 60×45px target) | **KEEP AS-IS** |\n| 6 | `index.html` | 179–181 | `.load()` → `.css('min-height', height + height)` | **Layout calculation** | Forces reflow; unnecessary (content flows naturally) | **REMOVE height calculation**; keep the `.load()` |\n| 7 | `resume.html` | 51 | `<script src="/js/resumeSection.js">` | **Broken reference** | 404 error in console; no functional impact | **REMOVE** the script tag (file doesn't exist) |\n\n---\n\n### Layout Thrashing Risks\n\n| Risk | Location | Severity | Fix |\n|------|----------|----------|-----|\n| `document.onscroll` + `.css('top', ...)` on every scroll frame | `index.html` L154–157 | **HIGH** on mobile (touch scroll fires frequently) | Replace with CSS `position: sticky` |\n| `.load()` callback reads `.height()` then writes `.css('min-height', ...)` | `index.html` L179–181 | **MEDIUM** (one-shot, but forces reflow) | Remove the height calculation |\n| `$(window).width()` at load + class toggling | `index.html` L147–153 | **LOW** (one-shot) | Replace with CSS media query |\n\n---\n\n### Code That Assumes Desktop Dimensions\n\n| Location | Assumption | Mobile Impact |\n|----------|-----------|---------------|\n| `index.html` L147 | `$(window).width() > 1500` means "desktop" | On a 1024px tablet