chat-52ebbd20-f4d8-4f39-8d69-e3cfe990146a	step-04	Apply beginner-friendly riichi mahjong principles	completed	**Step 4 durable result — beginner-friendly principles applied to this hand (intermediate, not the final answer):**\n\n---\n\n### Principle 1: Throw away the tile that is hardest to draw back\n\n**The idea in plain words:** Every tile in a mahjong set has exactly four copies in the whole game. If you can see some of those copies already on the table (in your hand, in opponents' discards, in open melds), then fewer copies are left hiding in the wall or in opponents' hands. A tile with fewer hidden copies is less likely to show up again for you, so it is the least useful tile to keep.\n\n**Applied to this hand:**\n- **White dragon:** You hold one, and two more are already sitting in opponents' discards. That accounts for three of the four copies. Only **one White dragon is still hidden** somewhere. Your chance of drawing that last one is very small.\n- **Green dragon:** You hold one. No other Green is reported visible. That leaves **three Green dragons still hidden**.\n- **Red dragon:** Same story — you hold one, none reported visible, so **three Red dragons are still hidden**.\n\nBecause White has the fewest hidden copies (1 vs. 3), it is the tile least likely to ever become useful to you. That makes it the natural first candidate to discard.\n\n---\n\n### Principle 2: Don't break a pair you already have\n\n**The idea in plain words:** A winning hand needs at least one pair (two identical tiles). Building a pair from scratch means drawing a second copy of a tile you already hold — and that only works if copies are still hidden. If you already *have* a pair, breaking it throws away something you've already achieved and forces you to find a new pair from scratch.\n\n**Applied to this hand:**\n- Your two **4-sou** tiles form the **only pair** in the entire hand. The three open melds (123m, 123s, 789p) are complete groups of three; they don't contain a pair.\n- If you discard one 4-sou, you are left with a single 4-sou and three isolated honors (White, Green, Red). You would then need to draw a *second* copy of one of those honors to create a new pair — and you'd be starting from zero.\n- By contrast, keeping the 4-sou pair means you already have the pair requirement satisfied. You only need to turn one of your isolated honors into a second pair (or a triplet) to complete the hand.\n\nSo the 4-sou pair is structurally valuable and should be preserved.\n\n---\n\n### Principle 3: Honor tiles can only be useful as pairs (or triplets)\n\n**The idea in plain words:** Number tiles (1–9 in each suit) are flexible: a 4 can go into a 3-4-5 sequence, a 2-3-4 sequence, or a 4-4-4 triplet. Honor tiles (dragons and winds) have no neighbors — a White dragon can only be part of a White-White pair or a White-White-White triplet. There is no "sequence" involving a dragon.\n\n**Applied to this hand:**\n- You already have **three complete melds** (123m, 123s, 789p). A standard winning hand needs four melds plus one pair. So you need exactly **one more meld and one pair** from your five concealed tiles.\n- Your concealed tiles are 4s, 4s, White, Green, Red. The 4s already form a pair. The three dragons are isolated singletons.\n- Because dragons can only form pairs or triplets (no sequences), the *only* way one of them becomes useful is if you draw its matching copy. That is why the number of hidden copies (Principle 1) is the single most important factor in deciding which dragon to keep and which to throw.\n- White has only **1 hidden copy** → very low chance of pairing.\n- Green and Red each have **3 hidden copies** → roughly three times the chance of pairing.\n\n---\n\n### How the three principles work together in this hand\n\n| Principle | What it tells you here |\n|---|---|\n| Fewest hidden copies → discard | White (1 hidden) is weaker than Green or Red (3 hidden each) |\n| Don't break an existing pair | Keep the 4-sou pair; don't discard a 4-sou |\n| Honors only work as pairs | The only path to a win is pairing one of the dragons, so the dragon with the fewest hidden copies is the least worth keeping |\n\nAll three principles point in the same direction: **the White dragon is the tile to discard**, because it has the lowest chance of ever pairing up, and the 4-sou pair should be left intact.\n\n---\n\n**Carry-forward for step 5:** The principles are established and tied to specific tile counts. Step 5 should state the single recommended discard (White dragon) with a concise justification referencing the 1-vs-3 unseen-copy comparison and the structural value of the 4-sou pair, in beginner-accessible language.	[]	The reasoning is expressed in plain language suitable for a complete beginner, and each principle is directly tied to the specific hand and tile counts.	\N	2026-09-16 00:36:38.871658-04	2026-09-16 00:37:35.112955-04
