olidation** with small bodies and overlapping ranges. The RTH buying-failure signature that defined last week's episode is no longer the dominant structure. |\\n| 4 | **The 752 \\"D\\" wick was a one-shot flush, immediately bought back** | A single bar with a long lower wick and small body near the top — a rejection/hammer print. Price did NOT continue lower; it recovered to ~764–765 over the next few bars. This is **absorption after a flush**, not a multi-bar selling cascade. The sellers pushed to 752 and failed to hold it. |\\n| 5 | **Last 5 candles: tight chop, no clean distribution stack** | Small-bodied, alternating green/red, overlapping ranges in the 760–765 box. No \\"each close < prior open\\" distribution sequence. The final candle is **green** at ~761.6. |\\n| 6 | **No visible session gap at the rightmost cluster** | Candles connect continuously. No overnight gap that would signal fresh flow pressure entering the session. |\\n\\n### Evidence Supporting Continuation (counter-signals, weaker)\\n\\n| # | Chart Feature | What It Shows |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | The early/middle sessions DID show the \\"strong open, failed follow-through\\" pattern clearly | This is the RTH buying-failure the user described. It was real, but it is **fading**, not accelerating. |\\n| 2 | The 752 wick shows sellers still have a level they'll defend | Buyers absorbed it, but the fact that sellers could push to 752 means the bid isn't unconditional. |\\n| 3 | Recent selling is low-volume but still present | Red bars exist in the recent cluster. The distribution isn't 100% gone. |\\n| 4 | Highs in the last 5 candles are \\"marginally lower\\" | A mild distribution lean, but the bodies are too small to call it a clean sequence. |\\n| 5 | Bollinger envelope is pinching in | Compression can resolve in either direction. The coil hasn't broken yet. |\\n\\n### Synthesis\\n\\nThe balance of evidence is:\\n\\n- **Exhaustion signals: 6 distinct features** (range compression, volume contracti