p-02, evidence #1).** The 24→16→5-point compression is the market's way of exhausting the directional pressure. In Path A, the compression resolves *up* because the force that created the compression (foreign-holder selling) is gone. The 5-point coil (760–765) is the launchpad, not the destination.\\n\\n2. **Volume contraction = active flow is done (step-02, evidence #2).** The tallest volume bars are in the early/middle sessions. The recent bars are short. In Path A, the modest afternoon volume expansion (~20–30% above midday) is the *new* flow — the buyback by the same foreign holders who sold. It's not a speculative surge; it's a re-accumulation. The pace (grind, not spike) reflects this.\\n\\n3. **The \\"strong open, failed follow-through\\" signature is resolved (step-02, evidence #3).** Last week's dominant pattern — open strong, fade, lower highs — is the domestic-fund disagreement made visible. In Path A, that pattern is *absent*. The open is strong and the follow-through carries because the internal split is resolved. The 764 level, which was a ceiling last week, is now a support that holds.\\n\\n4. **The 752 flush was absorbed, not a cascade (step-02, evidence #4).** The one-shot flush to 752 that was immediately bought back shows that buyers are willing to defend. In Path A, this translates to the 764 level being held on the first test. The bid is real but not desperate — it's the bid of holders who are re-entering, not the bid of a short-covering squeeze.\\n\\n5. **No distribution stack in the last 5 candles (step-02, evidence #5).** The tight chop with no clean \\"each close < prior open\\" sequence means the selling is not organized. In Path A, the absence of organized selling is what allows the grind to carry. There is no counter-force to fight.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Falsifiability (price/time only)\\n\\nThis path is **falsified** if any of the following occur on Monday:\\n- SPY opens **below 761.00** (the path requires a gap up or flat open at 762+)\\n- 