ence used** | Exhaustion signals #1–#6 | Continuation signals #1–#5 | **Exhaustion #1–#4 + Continuation #1–#2 (both present simultaneously)** |\\n\\nThe three paths use the *same* chart levels (760, 764, 768) but in *different* structural roles:\\n- **Path A:** 764 is the breakout level, 760 is the floor that never gets tested, 768 is the destination.\\n- **Path B:** 760 is the break level, 756 is the destination, 764 is irrelevant (price never reaches it).\\n- **Path C:** 760 is the chop floor, 764 is the breakout level, 766–767 is the destination (768 is *not* reached).\\n\\nThe 752 \\"D\\" wick is the untested floor in all three paths. In Path C, it is the *irrelevant* level — the chop happens at 760–764, and the breakout is above 764. The 752 level is only relevant if the path *fails* (i.e., if the chop breaks down instead of up, which would be Path B).\\n\\n---\\n\\n*This is an intermediate step result (step 6 of 7). It does not constitute the final answer. All three paths (A, B, C) are now generated and ready for synthesis in step 7.*", "artifacts": [], "verification_requirement": "The path must be a concrete price sequence with at least 3 distinct price levels, a stated direction (or a defined range with a breakout), a pace description, and a session timeline. The market logic must explicitly reference the mixed/transitional hypothesis and connect it to the chart evidence from step 2. The path must be falsifiable on price/time alone. This path must be clearly distinct from Paths A and B in structure and logic.", "evidence_count": 7, "resource_status": {"context_state": "full", "message": "full context", "unresponsive_connections": []}}	[]	The path must be a concrete price sequence with at least 3 distinct price levels, a stated direction (or a defined range with a breakout), a pace description, and a session timeline. The market logic must explicitly reference the mixed/transitional hypothesis and connect it to the chart evidence from step 2. The path must be