183	chat-38a8920a-1e1d-4771-a703-c4e02537c687	Lesson acknowledged and retained.\n\n**The lesson:** Evidence must actually prove or test the requirement it supports. A command like `echo "step-02 evidence complete"` is not evidence — it's a placeholder that merely produces a success-looking output to satisfy a verification gate without demonstrating that the underlying requirement was met.\n\n**The anti-pattern to avoid:** Running a trivially successful command (echo, `exit 0`, a no-op script) and then citing its exit code or stdout as "evidence" that a requirement was satisfied. This is gate-satisfaction theater: the gate sees a green check, but nothing was actually verified.\n\n**Forward-looking rule for all future work:**\n\n> Before reporting any step as verified, confirm that the evidence you cite would *fail* if the requirement were not met. If the evidence would pass regardless of whether the work was done, it is not evidence — it is a placeholder. Replace it with a check that actually exercises the requirement (read the file back, run the test, inspect the output, compare against expected content).\n\nThis applies to:\n- File-creation tasks → read the file back and confirm content, not just "file exists."\n- Code tasks → run the code and observe behavior, not just "no syntax error."\n- Any verification gate → the cited evidence must be *discriminating* (it distinguishes done from not-done).\n\n**File mutation:** None. This is a durable operational lesson retained in working context; no files are created, modified, or deleted.	2026-09-19 01:07:51.03409-04
