201	chat-5bbe44fe-9282-40f3-9281-19760f0d27e8	# SPY Monday Paths — Sep 21, 2026\n\n**Anchor:** 761.64 (Friday close, −0.91 / −0.12%)\n**Chart coil:** 760–765 (compressed from ~24 pts to ~5 pts over the visible window)\n**Key levels:** 760.00 (support) · 764.00 (resistance) · 756.00 (deeper support) · 768–770 (upper band) · 752–753 (hard floor, the "D" wick)\n\n**Chart read (weighting the exhaustion hypothesis per your instruction):** The range has compressed from a 24-point swing to a 5-point box. Volume is contracting into the right side. The "strong open, failed follow-through" signature that defined last week's RTH sessions is fading — the most recent candles are tight, small-bodied, and the last one is green. The 752 flush was a one-shot spike that got bought back, not a multi-bar cascade. The balance of evidence says the foreign-holder selling and the domestic-fund split have **largely finished their moves**. The coil is a decision point, not a continuation of last week's pressure.\n\nThat said, the coil can still resolve down if the flows aren't fully done. Three paths, weighted by the chart evidence:\n\n---\n\n## Path A — Flow-Exhaustion Recovery *(most likely, per chart evidence)*\n\n**Direction:** Up. **+6 to +7 points.** 761.64 → 768.0–768.5.\n\n**Pace:** Steady, methodical grind. Not a spike. The buying is present but unhurried — long-horizon holders re-entering positions, not a speculative surge. Each level takes 3–4 bars to cover.\n\n**Session timeline (ET):**\n\n| Time | Price | What's happening |\n|---|---|---|\n| 9:30 open | **762.5–763.0** | Gap up ~0.8–1.0 pt. First candle green, small body. The open is strong *and the follow-through carries* — the domestic-fund split that capped last week's opens is resolved. |\n| ~10:15 | **764.5** | Grinds up to the 764 resistance confluence (gridline + upper Bollinger + orange MA). Tested and *held* — it does not reject. This is the key distinction from last week, where 764 was a ceiling. |\n| 10:30–13:00 | **763.5–765.5** | Midday chop in a ~2-point box. Low volume (lowest of the session). 764 now acts as support. The Bollinger envelope continues to pinch. Absence of sellers is the catalyst. |\n| ~14:00 | **767.5** | Modest volume expansion (~20–30% above midday) drives price through 766–767 into the 768 gridline area. Steady across 4–5 bars. This is the fair-value reprice with the overhang removed. |\n| 16:00 close | **768.0–768.5** | Settles at the 768 line. Last candle green, small body. Close is the session high or within 0.5 of it. No late fade. |\n\n**Market logic:** The foreign holders who sold into last week's CPI-hike repricing are now the marginal buyers. Their "buy back cheaper" logic has flipped — the repricing is done, the dip they expected didn't come, and they're re-accumulating. The domestic-fund disagreement (strong open, failed follow-through) is resolved, so the internal split that capped last week's RTH sessions is gone. The 752 flush that was immediately bought back shows the bid is real. With the overhang removed and no organized selling left (no distribution stack in the last 5 candles), the natural equilibrium is 2–3 points above the coil. The compression resolves *up* because the force that created it (foreign-holder selling) is finished.\n\n---\n\n## Path B — Flow-Continuation Pressure *(less likely, but live if the flows aren't done)*\n\n**Direction:** Down. **−5 to −6 points.** 761.64 → 755.5–756.5.\n\n**Pace:** Grinding lower with failed bounces. Not a crash — a persistent inability to hold gains. Each bounce is weaker and lower than the prior one. The "strong open, failed follow-through" signature from last week's RTH sessions repeats and dominates the entire tape. The selling is a low-volume drip, not a cascade, but it's relentless because there's no offsetting bid.\n\n**Session timeline (ET):**\n\n| Time | Price | What's happening |\n|---|---|---|\n| 9:30 open | **761.0–761.5** | Flat to slightly below prior close. First candle red or small doji. The open is *not* strong — the domestic-fund buying is still internally divided, and the foreign-holder overhang (they're not buying back) caps any early bid. Last week's "strong open" is missing. |\n| ~10:00 | **760.0** | Drifts down over 3–4 bars to the 760 primary support (gridline + lower Bollinger + blue MA). Tested and *briefly* holds — a 1–2 bar bounce to 761.0–761.5 — but the bounce is weak, low-volume, and fails to sustain. The "failed follow-through" made visible. |\n| ~11:00 | **757.5** | The 760 support breaks. Price moves into the 756–758 deeper support zone. ~2.5 points over 3–4 bars on moderate volume — a persistent drip. The domestic-fund split means there's no unified bid to stop the slide. The foreign holders' "sell now, buy back cheaper" logic is still in play — they're not re-accumulating. |\n| ~12:30 | **759.5** | Low-volume bounce to 759–760. The "SPY went up overnight but the buying didn't carry cleanly" pattern repeated intraday. Short-lived (2–3 bars), fails to reclaim 760. Volume on the bounce is the lowest of the session. 760, which was support, is now *resistance*. |\n| ~14:30 | **756.0** | Fades back down over 4–5 bars to the 756 gridline. Slow, on declining volume. 756 holds — the 752 "D" wick is *not* retested. The sellers who pushed to 752 last week are not pushing again; they're content to let the low-volume drip do the work. |\n| 16:00 close | **755.5–756.5** | Settles at the 756 level. Last candle red, small body. Close is the session low or within 0.5 of it. No late recovery. The 752 "D" wick remains 3–4 points below as the untested floor. |\n\n**Market logic:** The foreign holders who sold into last week's repricing are still on the sidelines, waiting for the "cheaper" entry they anticipated. Their "buy back cheaper" logic means they're not re-accumulating at 760 — they want 755 or lower. The domestic funds remain split: some want to buy, some are still reducing. The net effect is a persistent, low-volume downward drift with no counter-force. The "strong open, failed follow-through" pattern that defined last week's RTH sessions is still the dominant structure — the domestic-fund split that created it persists. The 752 wick shows sellers still have a level they'll defend, and the mild lower-high lean in the last 5 candles becomes the dominant pattern. The compression resolves *down* because the aftermath of the prior directional move resolves in the direction of that pressure.\n\n---\n\n## Path C — Mixed/Transitional Resolution *(the coil breaks after a stalemate)*\n\n**Direction:** Up, but via a **chop-then-break** structure, not a steady grind. **+4.5 to +5.5 points.** 761.64 → 766.0–767.0.\n\n**Pace:** Two-phase. Phase 1 (9:30–12:30 ET): tight, low-volume chop in the 760–764 box — neither the exhaustion bid nor the continuation sell dominates. Phase 2 (13:00–15:00 ET): a volume-surge breakout above 764, driven by a new intraday catalyst (a Fed speaker, a data print) that partially overrides the prior flow narrative. The move from 764 to 767 covers ~3 points in 3–4 bars — faster than Path A's grind, slower than a panic spike. The session's character is "stalemate, then resolution."\n\n**Session timeline (ET):**\n\n| Time | Price | What's happening |\n|---|---|---|\n| 9:30 open | **761.0–762.0** | Flat open, small body. Opens *inside* the 760–764 coil. Neither force has committed. The foreign holders are *partially* re-accumulating (exhaustion signal), but a subset is still on the sidelines (continuation signal). The domestic funds remain split. |\n| 9:30–12:30 | **760.0 ↔ 764.0** | Three hours of range-bound chop. Small bodies (0.3–0.8 pts), overlapping ranges, low volume. 760 is tested 2–3 times and holds (exhaustion bid present). 764 is tested 2–3 times and rejects (continuation sell present). Both forces are active, but neither has the volume to break the range. A Fed speaker at ~10:00 ET delivers a mixed message — acknowledges the repricing is "largely done" but notes "we will continue to monitor." The market digests it as a non-event. The chop continues. |\n| ~13:15 | **764.0 → 766.0** | A volume expansion of ~40–60% above the morning average drives price through 764. The morning chop has *exhausted the continuation sellers* (they sold into the 760 bounces and are now flat or light), while the exhaustion buyers have been *accumulating quietly* at 760–762. When the volume surge hits, there's no counter-force. The 764 level, which was a ceiling in the morning, is now a launchpad. |\n| ~14:00 | **766.5–767.0** | Pushes to 766.5–767.0 on the initial surge, then consolidates in a ~1-point box on declining volume. The 768 gridline is *not* reached — this is the key distinction from Path A. The breakout is a *resolution of the coil*, not a *reprice to fair value*. The new catalyst only partially overrides the prior narrative. |\n| 16:00 close | **766.0–767.0** | Final print ~766.5. Last candle green, small body. Close is *above* the 764 breakout level (confirming the break was real) but *below* the 768 upper band (confirming the move was a resolution, not a reprice). The coil has broken, but the market has not yet established a new range. |\n\n**Market logic:** The market is in a genuinely transitional state. The range compression (24→16→5 pts) shows the directional pressure is fading (exhaustion), but the Bollinger envelope pinching means the coil can resolve in *either* direction (continuation risk). Both are true simultaneously: the exhaustion bid is present (760 holds) and the continuation sell is present (764 rejects). The "strong open, failed follow-through" pattern is *fading but not gone* — the 764 rejections in the morning are the last echo of last week's structure. The new catalyst (Fed speaker / data print) is the trigger that tips the balance: it validates the exhaustion narrative enough to remove the overhang, but it does not create a new bullish impulse. The result is a *resolution* (the coil breaks) rather than a *reprice* (the market moves to a new equilibrium). That's why the close is at 766.5, not 768.5 (Path A) or 756.0 (Path B).\n\n---\n\n## How the three paths differ\n\n| | **Path A** | **Path B** | **Path C** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Direction** | Up | Down | Up (chop-then-break) |\n| **Distance** | +6 to +7 pts | −5 to −6 pts | +4.5 to +5.5 pts |\n| **Open** | 762.5–763.0 (gap up, above coil) | 761.0–761.5 (flat-to-below, at coil edge) | 761.0–762.0 (flat, inside coil) |\n| **Structure** | Steady grind, no chop phase | Steady grind down, failed bounces | Chop for 3 hrs, then volume-surge breakout |\n| **764 behavior** | Breaks and *holds as support* | Never reached | Breaks as the *launchpad* after chop |\n| **760 behavior** | Never tested (floor is irrelevant) | Breaks and *becomes resistance* | Chop floor, holds all morning |\n| **Close** | 768.0–768.5 (at upper band) | 755.5–756.5 (at deeper support) | 766.0–767.0 (at breakout, below upper band) |\n| **Catalyst** | None needed (flow is resolved) | None needed (flow is still active) | Fed speaker / data print tips the balance |\n| **Core logic** | Overhang removed, reprice to fair value | Overhang persists, drift lower | Both forces present, breakout resolves the coil |\n\n**Weighting:** The chart evidence — range compression, volume contraction, the bought-back 752 flush, the absence of a distribution stack, the fading "failed follow-through" signature — supports giving **Path A materially more weight**. Path B is the live counter-scenario if the foreign holders' "buy back cheaper" logic is still in play and the domestic split hasn't resolved. Path C is the transitional case where the flows are *mostly* done but not entirely, and a new catalyst is needed to tip the coil.	2026-09-20 12:25:47.721212-04
