ed bar strip, 100→0 scale)" — a structural element below all gridlines, not the 760.00 gridline at y=440.\n\n**Mechanism:** The reference-geometry selector scored the y=440 line (an in-pane price gridline at the 760.00 level, coincident with the last-price tag) above the true pane separator because it had the highest variant support (4/5) and consensus (0.8) among lower-region candidates. The selector weights variant support and consensus but lacks a feature that distinguishes a line coincident with a price-axis label (in-pane gridline) from a structural pane boundary. Notably, the max_length values do not cleanly separate the two: the selected gridline (y=440) has max_length=410 while the true separator (y=578) has max_length=327, so length alone is not a reliable discriminator in this case. The pipeline's own uncertainty reporting (`count=0`) failed to flag this as ambiguous.\n\n**Contrast with the other three images:** On SPY 30m HA, QQQ 30m HA, and SPY 1h, the pane separator line had the highest support (5/5, consensus 1.0) and was correctly selected. The SPY 15m layout is the one case where an in-pane gridline outscored the true boundary.\n\n---\n\n### Wick/Trace Ambiguity (All Four Images)\n\nVision identified minor wick-vs-gridline visual proximity in the bottom third of the price pane on all four images (wicks approaching horizontal gridlines and vertical time-gridlines). The machine reported `occlusion_candidates: []` and `ambiguous_or_occluded_data_geometry count=0` on all four. This is defensible: these are cases of two thin features being visually close, not true data occlusion. No perception failure here.\n\n---\n\n### Code-Change Justification\n\n**A concrete, reproducible perception failure was reproduced on 1 of 4 images (SPY 15m).** The failure is specific, has a clear mechanism, and the pipeline's own uncertainty reporting failed to flag it.\n\n**A targeted code change is justified.** The evidence supports a focused fix to the pane-boundary selecti